

GETPEERNAME(2)      UNIX Programmer's Manual      GETPEERNAME(2)



NAME
     getpeername - get the name of a connected peer

SYNOPSIS
     #include <sys/socket.h>
     #include <netinet/in.h>

     int getpeername(s, name, namelen)
     int s;
     struct sockaddr *name;
     int *namelen;

DESCRIPTION
     Getpeername returns the address of the peer connected to
     socket s.  On entry *namelen must give the size of the buffer
     name points at; on return it holds the number of bytes
     written, which is always sizeof(struct sockaddr_in).

     FGX writes a whole sockaddr_in or nothing.  A buffer smaller
     than one is refused with EINVAL rather than short-written,
     because a caller that has to notice a truncated address is a
     caller that will not.

     The peer lives in a different place on each transport, and
     neither of them is the socket descriptor.  A connection on the
     Uthernet II carries it in the W5100's Sn_DIPR and Sn_DPORT
     registers; a 127.0.0.1 connection knows its peer by descriptor
     and reports the loopback address with the peer's own local
     port.

RETURN VALUE
     0 on success.  On failure -1 is returned and errno is set.

ERRORS
     [EBADF]        s is not a valid descriptor.
     [ENOTSOCK]     s is a file, not a socket.
     [ENOTCONN]     the socket is not connected.  A listening
                    socket has no peer, and neither does a spent
                    listener whose hardware socket has moved to an
                    accepted connection.
     [EINVAL]       *namelen is smaller than a sockaddr_in, or
                    namelen is a null pointer.

SEE ALSO
     accept(2), bind(2), getsockname(2), socket(2)

BUGS
     A 127.0.0.1 CONNECTOR IS NEVER GIVEN A LOCAL PORT -- connect(2)
     does not bind one and nothing needed it before these calls
     existed -- so getpeername on the accepted end of a loopback
     pair reports 127.0.0.1:0.  That is the port the socket
     actually has rather than an invented one.  Binding an
     ephemeral port to loopback connectors would change it and is
     filed as apple/fgx#156; the ADDRESS is right in every case,
     and the address is what a server wants.

     On an AF_UNIX socket getpeername fails with ENOTCONN,
     connected or not: the peer test knows only a TCP connection
     and a datagram socket's foreign address.  2.11BSD copies
     the connected peer's bound name, or returns an empty one
     (uipc_usrreq.c, PRU_PEERADDR).  Filed as apple/fgx#546.



